  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 32,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "index": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.046,
            0.367,
            0.064
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Ganita Prakash | Grade 7"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.087,
            0.848,
            0.173
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "If you watch cricket, you might have noticed decimal-looking numbers like ‘Overs left: 5.5’. Does this mean 5 overs and 5 balls or 5 overs and 3 balls? Here, 5.5 overs means "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "5   \\frac { 5 } { 6 }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " overs (as 1 over = 6 balls), i.e., 5 overs and 5 balls."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.181,
            0.772,
            0.22
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Where else can we see such ‘non-decimals’ with a decimal-like notation?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.818,
            0.17,
            0.905,
            0.222
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 3,
              "bbox": [
                0.818,
                0.17,
                0.905,
                0.222
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_32_image_3.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.243,
            0.746,
            0.266
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "A Pinch of History–Decimal Notation Over Time"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.277,
            0.848,
            0.455
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Decimal fractions (i.e., fractions with denominators like "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\frac{1}{10} \\text { , } \\frac{1}{100}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\frac { 1 } { 1 0 0 0 }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " , and so on) are used in the works of a number of ancient Indian astronomers and mathematicians, including in the important 8th century works of Śhrīdharāchārya on arithmetic and algebra. Decimal notation, in essentially its modern form, was described in detail in Kitāb al-Fuṣūl fī al-Ḥisāb al Hindī (The Book of Chapters on Indian Arithmetic) by Abūl Ḥassan al-Uqlīdisī, an Arab mathematician, in around 950 CE. He represented the number 0.059375 as 0ˈ 059375."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.454,
            0.847,
            0.492
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In the 15th century, to separate whole numbers from fractional parts, a number of different notations were used:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.173,
            0.496,
            0.847,
            0.534
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "a vertical mark on the last digit of the whole number part (as shown above),"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.174,
            0.538,
            0.482,
            0.558
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "• use of different colours and"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.174,
            0.563,
            0.847,
            0.611
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "a numerical superscript giving the number of fractional decimal 2 places (0.36 would be written as 36)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.616,
            0.848,
            0.69
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In the 16th century, John Napier, a Scottish mathematician, and Christopher Clavius, a German mathematician, used the point/period (‘.’) to separate the whole number and the fractional parts, while François Viète, a French mathematician, used the comma (‘,’) instead."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.69,
            0.848,
            0.784
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Currently, several countries use the comma to separate the integer part and the fractional part. In these countries, the number 1,000.5 is written as 1 000,5 (space as a thousand separator). But the decimal point has endured as the most popular notation for writing numbers having fractional parts in the Indian place value system."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.79,
            0.293,
            0.814
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Figure it Out"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.816,
            0.786,
            0.887
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "1. Convert the following fractions into decimals: (a) "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\frac { 5 } { 1 0 0 }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " (b) "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\frac { 1 6 } { 1 0 0 0 }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " (c) "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\frac { 1 2 } { 1 0 }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " (d) "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\frac { 2 5 4 } { 1 0 0 0 }"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.16,
            0.938,
            0.187,
            0.955
          ],
